# Biome 1, level 11 — Trade Winds
#
# Trade arrives, and with it the first board where two kinds run the same way down the same slope
# rather than crossing. Neither route is in the other's way, so the difficulty is entirely in the
# hand: half the tiles drawn belong to the other network, and a tile of the wrong kind is worth
# nothing at the wrong end of the board.
#
# The target is 420 against a best of 428 — the four-conduit water line and the three-conduit trade
# line, both of them, with almost nothing spare.
#
# The board started as a fifteen-cell field with the routes drawn through the middle of it. The
# solvability gate could not prove it in 600,000 states: open cells multiply the search without
# adding a decision, because a conduit placed in the empty part of the board is never part of an
# answer. Trimming it to the routes and one spare cell made the level provable and no easier.

id: biome1-11
name: Trade Winds

base-score: 100
target-score: 420
tokens: 0
skips: 3

cell: 0,-2
cell: 2,-2
cell: 0,-1
cell: 1,-1
cell: 2,-1
cell: 0,0
cell: 1,0
cell: 0,1
cell: 1,1
cell: -2,2
cell: -1,2
cell: 0,2

spring: 0,-2 water
hub: -2,2 water
spring: 2,-2 trade
hub: 0,2 trade

tile: 0,3 water x3
tile: 0,2 water x3
tile: 0,2 trade x3
